from p4lib import Doc

d = Doc()

d.para("Anomaly detectors for rolling-element-bearing prognostics are typically evaluated",
       "Anomaly detectors for rolling-element-bearing prognostics are usually scored by classification accuracy, which shows whether a fault is detected but not how early or at what false-alarm cost. We make lead time, the warning interval before failure, the primary evaluation object through a latch-on-resistant, false-alarm-gated metric: warning time is credited only when the pre-onset false-alarm rate lies within a stated budget $\\tau$, so a degenerate constant-on detector is provably scored invalid. We use it to ask whether the bin-averaged logging of a SCADA historian destroys detection lead time relative to simple decimation. Eleven detectors (four statistical process control (SPC) charts, Isolation Forest, three deep reconstruction autoencoders, a root-mean-square (RMS) trend baseline and two one-class models) are evaluated under a controlled sampling sweep on four inferential run-to-failure datasets: XJTU-SY ($n=10$), FEMTO/PRONOSTIA ($n=6$), University of Ferrara ($n=6$) and NASA IMS ($n=3$). On IMS the variance-sensitive charts show a positive median shift ($3\\sigma$ $+15.1$~h) that is not consistent across the three runs, and no test survives Holm correction across the $N=44$ family (0 of 44; smallest adjusted $p=1.00$). Against a pre-specified $\\pm 1$~h margin---the resolution at which a maintenance planner can act, since sub-hour differences in warning time are not actionable---the run-level aggregate-minus-decimate difference is equivalent to zero in all 33 dataset $\\times$ detector cells of the three multi-bearing campaigns, and no cell shows decimation superior beyond it: the widest interval favouring decimation ($3\\sigma$ on FEMTO) reaches only $-0.45$~h. On IMS the intervals are too wide at $n=3$ to decide in 9 of 11 cells and favour aggregation beyond the margin in the other 2; a single-asset gas-turbine record of the Oil and Natural Gas Corporation (ONGC, $n=1$) cannot be tested. The campaigns that supply the statistical power are short-lived bearings, on which an effect of the IMS magnitude could not have appeared, so the null bounds the effect at the scale of minutes rather than hours. On these bearings deep reconstruction models do not overtake the control charts at any training fraction in $[0.20, 0.60]$; whether they do on long-runway assets is left open. Pipeline, detectors and result tables: \\nburl{https://github.com/Ansh-Goyal01/Scada-Leadtime-Benchmark}.")

d.para("Condition-based maintenance (CBM) and prognostics and health management (PHM) promise",
       "Condition-based maintenance (CBM) and prognostics and health management (PHM) replace fixed-interval servicing with data-driven intervention, reducing both unplanned downtime and unnecessary maintenance \\cite{jardine2006review,lee2014prognostics,lei2018machinery}. Rolling-element bearings are the most common failure mode in rotating machinery, and statistical control charts, classical machine learning and deep models are all used to detect incipient bearing faults from vibration \\cite{randall2011rolling,zhao2019deep}. A warning delivered hours or days before a catastrophic seizure lets an operator schedule an orderly shutdown, order parts and avoid secondary damage to shafts, housings and couplings.")

d.para("Despite this, most published detectors are evaluated with classification metrics",
       "Yet most published detectors are evaluated with classification metrics---accuracy, precision/recall and receiver operating characteristic area under the curve (ROC--AUC)---which answer ``was the failure detected?'' but not the planner's question: ``how many hours of warning did we get, and how often did the system cry wolf?'' A detector that warns 48~h ahead at a low nuisance-alarm rate is operationally distinct from one that fires minutes before breakdown or one that alarms every day. Time-aware prognostic metrics have long been advocated \\cite{saxena2008metrics,saxena2010metrics,goebel2011comparison}, yet lead time is rarely the headline quantity in detector benchmarks, and ad hoc definitions are easy to game: without a false-alarm constraint, a detector that alarms from the first sample---a ``latch-on'' exploit---earns the full run length as lead time while being operationally useless.")

d.para("A second, eminently practical question motivates this study.",
       "A second question is practical. Industrial Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA) historians rarely store raw high-rate vibration; they keep decimated or time-averaged summaries at a logging interval of seconds to minutes \\cite{tautzweinert2017using}, not the tens of kilohertz at which accelerometers sample. The folklore concern is that bin-averaging these summaries washes out the excursions that signal an incipient spall, so that long-term storage would blind any prognostic algorithm that consumes the historian. To our knowledge this has not been tested with a sound lead-time metric across multiple datasets. If averaging is harmless, operators can store compact, cheap, bin-averaged streams without sacrificing warning time; if it is harmful, prognostics-grade monitoring requires separate high-rate acquisition.")

d.para("This paper makes both questions precise and answers them.",
       "We answer both questions. Lead time gated by a leakage-free pre-onset false-alarm rate is the primary metric, so the latch-on detector is provably scored invalid rather than winning. Under it we run a controlled sampling sweep that holds window content and alarm persistence constant, over nine headline detectors (four SPC charts, Isolation Forest, three deep reconstruction autoencoders and an RMS-trend baseline) plus two additionally evaluated one-class models, Deep SVDD (deep support vector data description) and a one-class SVM, on four inferential run-to-failure datasets---XJTU-SY ($n=10$), FEMTO/PRONOSTIA ($n=6$), University of Ferrara ($n=6$) and NASA IMS ($n=3$)---and a real single-asset ONGC gas-turbine SCADA record analyzed as a case study (Appendix~D.2), with run-level significance tests (the run, not the within-run sampling factor, is the unit of inference) and Holm correction. The folklore is refuted on every dataset tested: historian-style aggregation does not destroy lead time. The difference is negligible on the multi-bearing sets, positive but inconsistent in direction on IMS, and consistent with non-destruction on the turbine record. The defensible conclusion is bounded non-destruction: no dataset $\\times$ detector cell shows decimation superior beyond a $\\pm 1$~h operational margin, the resolution at which a planner can act (Section~\\ref{sec:femto}).")
for gone in ("\\textbf{Problem.}", "\\textbf{Solution.}", "\\textbf{Method.}", "\\textbf{Finding.}"):
    d.drop(gone)
d.para("\\textbf{Contributions.} We make five contributions.", "\\textbf{Contributions.}")

d.para("\\item \\emph{A latch-on--resistant, false-alarm-gated, onset-relative lead-time metric.}",
       "\\item \\emph{A latch-on--resistant, false-alarm-gated, onset-relative lead-time metric}, crediting warning time only when the false-alarm rate over a data-anchored, leakage-free pre-onset region is within a stated budget. A constant-on detector is provably invalid and one firing just after the true onset is valid at zero false-alarm rate, by construction and by a released unit test. We do not claim the metric is unconditionally ungameable (Section~\\ref{sec:limitations}).")
d.para("\\item \\emph{A formal proof that gating changes the answer.}",
       "\\item \\emph{A formal comparison showing that gating changes the answer}, to our knowledge the first: the ungated Saxena prognostic horizon (PH) makes a different and operationally misleading recommendation (Section~\\ref{sec:saxena}). On IMS the seven detectors PH ranks highest---the deep models, the one-class SVM, Hotelling $T^2$, Isolation Forest and the exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA) chart---have no valid operating point at a 10\\% budget; the gated metric picks $3\\sigma$, PH's eighth.")
d.para("\\item \\emph{A controlled SCADA-sampling methodology}",
       "\\item \\emph{A controlled SCADA-sampling methodology} holding window content and alarm persistence constant, so an aggregate-vs-decimate difference reflects information loss rather than a window-counting artifact of coarser time grids.")
d.para("\\item \\emph{A multi-dataset null result.}",
       "\\item \\emph{A multi-dataset null result}: with bootstrap confidence intervals and run-level tests, SCADA-rate aggregation does not destroy lead time on any of the four inferential datasets, and nothing survives Holm correction across the $N=44$ family.")
d.para("\\item \\emph{Validation:} wiring and empirical validation",
       "\\item \\emph{Validation} of a conformal detector for distribution-free false-alarm control; a lead-time--vs--false-alarm trade-off analysis; feature-group and spectral ablations that localize the prognostic signal to time-domain statistics on IMS; and deep reconstruction models (an LSTM autoencoder, a temporal convolutional network autoencoder (TCN-AE) and a transformer anomaly detector) that do not become competitive with SPC charts at training fractions in $[0.20, 0.60]$ on short-lived bearings, a crossover on long-runway assets remaining open (Section~\\ref{sec:mintrain}).")
d.para("\\textbf{Scope of claims.}",
       "\\textbf{Scope of claims.} The results are for rolling-element bearings under approximately steady operating conditions; we make no claim of transfer to gearboxes, blades or variable-speed machinery, and the sampling question is specifically bin-averaged versus decimated historian storage. The metric itself is failure-mode-agnostic: it applies wherever a run-to-failure trajectory and a stationary normal region exist. Our working hypothesis was that bin-averaging the stored summary series would wash out incipient-fault excursions and shorten lead time. It does not, and we report the refutation, with its dataset-dependent direction explicit, as more valuable than a tidy but unsupported positive claim.")
d.drop("We deliberately report a result that is not the one we set out to find.")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Related Work
d.para("Reviews of machinery diagnostics and prognostics",
       "Reviews of machinery diagnostics and prognostics \\cite{jardine2006review,lee2014prognostics,lei2018machinery} and of remaining-useful-life (RUL) estimation \\cite{si2011remaining} establish the CBM/PHM framing in which a health indicator is tracked and an alarm or RUL estimate is produced. \\citeA{saxena2008metrics}, \\citeA{saxena2010metrics} and \\citeA{goebel2011comparison} propose prognostic-performance metrics (prognostic horizon, $\\alpha$--$\\lambda$ accuracy, relative accuracy and timeliness) that reward early, well-calibrated predictions. Our onset-relative lead time is in this spirit: it measures warning time relative to a degradation event, not an arbitrary positional marker. We depart from prior practice by making the false-alarm budget part of the metric's validity condition rather than a separately reported number, which closes the latch-on loophole, and we give what is to our knowledge the first formal comparison showing that an ungated prognostic horizon ranks detectors differently, and misleadingly, from a false-alarm-budgeted metric (Section~\\ref{sec:saxena}).")
d.para("The cost asymmetry between a missed detection and a false alarm",
       "The cost asymmetry between a missed detection and a false alarm has been treated explicitly outside bearing prognostics. The Numenta Anomaly Benchmark \\cite{lavin2015nab} scores streaming detectors under an application profile that rewards early detection and penalises false positives, making our gate's commitment in another domain: earliness cannot be assessed independently of nuisance-alarm behaviour. The difference is structural. That benchmark folds both into one weighted score, so a detector may trade false alarms against earliness and still rank well; our budget is a validity condition, and an alarm outside it earns no credit that a longer lead can offset.")
d.para("A parallel line of work revises evaluation itself",
       "A parallel line of work revises evaluation itself for time-series detection. \\citeA{tatbul2018} generalise precision and recall from points to ranges with a tunable positional bias, so early detection within an anomalous interval can be rewarded, and \\citeA{wu2023flawed} argue that widely used benchmarks admit trivial solutions and overstate progress. Our metric shares the diagnosis but reports a physical quantity---hours of warning---that a planner can act on, rather than a score on a bounded scale, and the false-alarm constraint gates validity rather than weighting the score.")
d.para("Univariate and multivariate statistical-process-control charts",
       "Univariate and multivariate statistical-process-control charts---the $3\\sigma$ rule, the exponentially weighted moving average (EWMA) \\cite{roberts1959control} and Hotelling's $T^2$ \\cite{hotelling1947multivariate}---remain workhorses of industrial monitoring \\cite{montgomery2009introduction} because they are interpretable and need little training data. Isolation Forest \\cite{liu2008isolation} and the one-class support vector machine (SVM) \\cite{scholkopf2001estimating} are widely deployed unsupervised learners; LSTM autoencoders \\cite{hochreiter1997long,malhotra2016lstm,hundman2018detecting} and Deep SVDD \\cite{ruff2018deep} have been applied to machine-health monitoring \\cite{zhao2019deep}. We evaluate both families under one fixed hyperparameter protocol, so the comparison is about evaluation and sampling, not tuning.")
d.para("\\emph{Relation to published FEMTO/XJTU results.}",
       "\\emph{Relation to published FEMTO/XJTU results.} FEMTO/PRONOSTIA and XJTU-SY are best known through the IEEE PHM 2012 challenge and later remaining-useful-life (RUL) studies \\cite{wang2020hybrid,nectoux2012pronostia}, which report a RUL-error score on deliberately truncated Test/Validation copies. Those numbers are not directly comparable to ours: (i) they estimate a continuous RUL on runs with no observed failure, whereas we measure early-warning lead time on complete run-to-failure trajectories gated on a pre-onset false-alarm budget; and (ii) they report no false-alarm rate, so a RUL score says nothing about whether an alarm would survive our $\\tau$ gate. Tabulating them against our lead times would be a category error. The classic normalized-RMS health indicator with a first-prediction-time threshold, the baseline underlying most of that literature, is our rms\\_trend detector; re-evaluating specific published architectures under the gated metric (which requires their original code) is future work.")
d.para("The pairing of detection delay with a false-alarm constraint",
       "The pairing of detection delay with a false-alarm constraint is the founding formulation of sequential change detection. \\citeA{lorden1971} posed the minimax problem of minimising worst-case detection delay subject to a lower bound on the average run length to false alarm, \\citeA{moustakides1986} showed that the cumulative sum (CUSUM) chart of \\citeA{page1954continuous} is exactly optimal under that criterion, and \\citeA{pollak1985} gives the corresponding result for a revised delay measure. Our gate inherits the premise but applies it when a detector is \\emph{scored}, against a rate measured on the data, rather than when it is \\emph{designed} by choosing a threshold that meets a run-length bound. The two can disagree: CUSUM has no valid operating point on IMS at a 10\\% budget, because its pre-onset false-alarm rate sits at 19.0--19.8\\% across every threshold we sweep.")
d.para("Conformal prediction \\cite{vovk2005algorithmic,laxhammar2014online} provides",
       "Conformal prediction \\cite{vovk2005algorithmic,laxhammar2014online} bounds the false-alarm rate by a target $\\alpha$ under exchangeability, converting a raw score into an alarm with an interpretable, finite-sample guarantee. We wire conformal calibration into the lead-time pipeline and test the guarantee empirically across runs, including a run where exchangeability provably fails because the ``normal'' region already contains degradation.")
d.para("Bearing-defect frequencies, spectral kurtosis",
       "Bearing-defect frequencies, spectral kurtosis \\cite{antoni2006spectral} and envelope (demodulation) analysis \\cite{randall2011rolling} are the classical frequency-domain tools for bearing diagnostics. We include fast Fourier transform (FFT) band energies, spectral kurtosis and Hilbert-envelope amplitudes at the bearing characteristic frequencies, and ablate them against time-domain statistics to see which family carries prognostic lead time under the gated metric.")
d.para("\\citeA{tautzweinert2017using} review SCADA-data condition monitoring",
       "\\citeA{tautzweinert2017using} review SCADA-data condition monitoring and note that historian aggregation is a recognized but under-quantified concern: practitioners suspect that coarse averaging hurts fault detection, but the effect has not been measured with a principled warning-time metric. We address this gap and find that, for bearing faults, the concern is not borne out.")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Datasets
d.para("We use four inferential run-to-failure datasets spanning",
       "We use four inferential run-to-failure datasets and a single-asset real-historian case study (Table~\\ref{tab:datasets}), chosen so the sampling claim is tested under different conditions: a high-rate raw-waveform rig where aggregation can be simulated faithfully (IMS), two multi-bearing campaigns (XJTU-SY and FEMTO/PRONOSTIA) that supply the statistical power a three-run rig cannot, a second modern constant-condition rig (Ferrara), and a historian stream that already is SCADA-rate data (ONGC, Appendix~D.2). XJTU-SY ($n=10$) is the primary inferential dataset; IMS ($n=3$) is retained for its raw-waveform access, which permits the faithful aggregate/decimate simulation and the mechanism analysis (Appendix~D.1).")
d.drop("XJTU-SY ($n=10$) is our primary inferential dataset;")
d.para("\\emph{IMS.} The NASA/IMS bearing dataset",
       "\\emph{IMS.} The NASA/IMS dataset \\cite{qiu2006wavelet} provides raw 20.48~kHz accelerometer waveforms for three run-to-failure tests on a shaft loaded with four bearings, so frequency-domain features (spectral kurtosis, envelope) are meaningful; the coarsening sweep itself acts on the windowed feature vectors, not the waveform (Section~\\ref{sec:coarsenlevel}). The failure label of IMS test 3 is set as documented in the released repository and justified in Appendix~C.")
d.para("\\emph{XJTU-SY.} The XJTU-SY dataset",
       "\\emph{XJTU-SY.} The XJTU-SY dataset \\cite{wang2020hybrid} contributes ten run-to-failure bearings under two operating conditions (five each) at 25.6~kHz, in 1.28~s snapshots once per minute. With $n=10$ it gives the strongest generalization test here; its lifetimes range widely (52--533~min), stressing the metric with both long-degrading and abrupt-failure bearings.")
d.para("\\emph{FEMTO/PRONOSTIA.} The FEMTO/PRONOSTIA platform",
       "\\emph{FEMTO/PRONOSTIA.} The FEMTO/PRONOSTIA platform \\cite{nectoux2012pronostia} contributes six Learning\\_set bearings under three operating conditions, with horizontal and vertical accelerometers sampled at 25.6~kHz in 0.1~s snapshots every 10~s. Only the Learning\\_set bearings run to the platform's 20~g vibration stop, so a failure, and hence a lead time, is observed; the Test\\_set/Validation\\_set copies are truncated mid-life for the IEEE PHM 2012 RUL challenge and would leave lead time undefined. Lifetimes span ${\\sim}1.4$--7.8~h. We exclude the Paderborn dataset \\cite{lessmeier2016condition}: its pre-damaged bearings are measured at fixed conditions for fault classification, with no degradation timeline and no failure event, so it cannot test a lead-time or sampling claim, and using it would be a category error rather than a larger dataset count.")
d.para("\\emph{University of Ferrara.} The University of Ferrara dataset",
       "\\emph{University of Ferrara.} The Ferrara dataset \\cite{arpa2024ferrara} contributes an independent 2024 campaign: six self-aligning double-row ball bearings under constant speed and radial load, with continuous uniaxial vibration at 25.6~kHz over each bearing's entire lifecycle (five-second contiguous snapshots). Lifetimes span 0.7--6.8~h, similar to FEMTO. Fixed operating conditions make it directly compatible with our onset detector without normalization; we use all six runs (E1--E6), duplicating the single channel into the two channel slots so the channel-invariant schema applies unchanged. Like FEMTO and XJTU-SY, its short lifetimes mean it cannot confirm or deny the IMS positive trend.")

d.save()
